At a Usenix conference a couple years ago some folks presented a talk on a
package they wrote called DREGS, Distributed Real Time Environment for Game
Systems (or something like that). Two of their sample games were a multiplayer
BattleZone-style game, and multiplayer version of Hack 1.03.

I contacted the authors and got the source code for all this stuff, but never
did much with it. DREGS was built on X10R4; I dunno how much it would need to
be overhauled to work on X11. It would be nice to get distributed Hack working
again, but it'd also be nicer if an X server were not a requirement... 

Lessee, one feature I recall was that the game was real time, so if you just
sat around doing nothing, various monsters would walk up and pound on you. If
you saved the game, you would turn into an indestructible statue. Nothing
could harm you or take your items while thus saved, but sufficiently strong
critters could pick you up and move you.... Of course, there was plenty of
room for abuse, like saving the game when you realize the monster you
encountered could turn you into toast, and starting a new game with another
character to drop by and draw the monster away, or just saving to avoid a
fellow player's attack. But, it's hard to find a perfect solution...
